<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henri Giraud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henri Giraud was a French general who escaped from German captivity in both world wars, his escape from Königstein fortress in 1942 making him famous. After the Allied landings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> he became French high commissioner and commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, and in 1943 he co-chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-committee-of-national-liberation">French Committee of National Liberation</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> until he was sidelined later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-giraud.jpg" alt="Henri Giraud" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henri_Giraud_1943Jan19.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henri Honoré Giraud was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 18 January 1879. He graduated from Saint-Cyr in 1900 and at his own request joined the 4th Zouaves Regiment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he was gravely wounded leading a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> charge at the Battle of St. Quentin on 30 August 1914 and taken prisoner. With the help of a French nurse he escaped from the hospital at Origny-Sainte-Benoite with Captain Charles Schmitt on the night of 30 October, crossed into the neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> with the help of Edith Cavell's escape network and reached Paris in February 1915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Giraud served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rif-war">Rif War</a> in 1925 and from 1930 to 1934 pacified the tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a>. In 1936 he became military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, where Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> served under him from 1937. In 1940 he took command of the disintegrating Ninth Army on 15 May and was captured at Wassigny four days later. He was held at Königstein Fortress, and after he escaped to Vichy France in April 1942 he made contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, who preferred him as the French leader.</p><p>After the Allied landings of November 1942 he took command of French troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and, after the assassination of François Darlan on 24 December, became High Commissioner for French North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. He retained Vichy-era legislation, but in a speech in March 1943 he broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime. On 3 June 1943 he and de Gaulle agreed to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-committee-of-national-liberation">French Committee of National Liberation</a> as co-presidents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">De Gaulle</a> gained control of the political situation, and on 9 November 1943 signed a decree removing Giraud from the committee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In June 1946 Giraud was elected to the Constituent Assembly for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> and voted against the constitution of the Fourth Republic. He published Mes évasions in 1946 and Un seul but, la victoire in 1949. He died in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> military hospital on 11 March 1949, the day after he received the Médaille militaire for his escape of 1942. A state funeral followed, and he was interred in the vault of the Cathedral of Saint-Louis-des-Invalides in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henri Giraud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…at Algiers and Oran in Operation Torch, and in 1943 Algiers became the seat of the French Committee of National Liberation, headed jointly by Charles de Gaulle and <b>Henri Giraud</b> until de Gaulle took sole control; Algerian units then fought in Italy and in the liberation of France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>That year the movement moved to Algiers and merged with <b>Henri Giraud's</b> forces in the French Committee of National Liberation.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-france">Free France</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1924" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henri Giraud"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1924">What was here in 1924? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
